{
  "sequence": [
    {
      "type": "P",
      "id": "eyebrow",
      "text": "CELL BIOLOGY  /  THE CELL CYCLE"
    },
    {
      "type": "H1",
      "id": "title",
      "text": "How cells reproduce (mitosis)"
    },
    {
      "type": "P",
      "id": "subtitle",
      "text": "One cell copies its DNA, then splits into two daughter cells that carry the same chromosomes."
    },
    {
      "type": "H2",
      "id": "group_growth",
      "text": "GROW + COPY DNA"
    },
    {
      "type": "P",
      "id": "interphase_step",
      "text": "01"
    },
    {
      "type": "Figure",
      "id": "asset_interphase",
      "alt": "A round cell with a large purple nucleus, before it divides."
    },
    {
      "type": "H3",
      "id": "interphase_title",
      "text": "Interphase"
    },
    {
      "type": "P",
      "id": "interphase_desc",
      "text": "The cell grows and copies its DNA: each chromosome becomes two sister chromatids."
    },
    {
      "type": "H2",
      "id": "group_mitosis",
      "text": "MITOSIS  /  THE NUCLEUS DIVIDES"
    },
    {
      "type": "P",
      "id": "prophase_step",
      "text": "02"
    },
    {
      "type": "Figure",
      "id": "asset_prophase",
      "alt": "A cell whose chromosomes have coiled into X shapes inside a dissolving, dashed nuclear envelope."
    },
    {
      "type": "H3",
      "id": "prophase_title",
      "text": "Prophase"
    },
    {
      "type": "P",
      "id": "prophase_desc",
      "text": "Chromosomes coil up into visible X shapes and the spindle begins to form."
    },
    {
      "type": "P",
      "id": "metaphase_step",
      "text": "03"
    },
    {
      "type": "Figure",
      "id": "asset_metaphase",
      "alt": "A cell with three X-shaped chromosomes lined up across the middle, held by spindle fibers from a pole on each side."
    },
    {
      "type": "H3",
      "id": "metaphase_title",
      "text": "Metaphase"
    },
    {
      "type": "P",
      "id": "metaphase_desc",
      "text": "Spindle fibers line the chromosomes up across the middle of the cell."
    },
    {
      "type": "P",
      "id": "anaphase_step",
      "text": "04"
    },
    {
      "type": "Figure",
      "id": "asset_anaphase",
      "alt": "An oval cell with the sister chromatids being pulled apart toward opposite poles."
    },
    {
      "type": "H3",
      "id": "anaphase_title",
      "text": "Anaphase"
    },
    {
      "type": "P",
      "id": "anaphase_desc",
      "text": "The sister chromatids are pulled apart toward opposite ends of the cell."
    },
    {
      "type": "P",
      "id": "telophase_step",
      "text": "05"
    },
    {
      "type": "Figure",
      "id": "asset_telophase",
      "alt": "A cell pinched in at the middle, with a new nucleus forming at each end."
    },
    {
      "type": "H3",
      "id": "telophase_title",
      "text": "Telophase"
    },
    {
      "type": "P",
      "id": "telophase_desc",
      "text": "A new nuclear envelope forms around each of the two sets of chromosomes."
    },
    {
      "type": "H2",
      "id": "group_split",
      "text": "THE CELL SPLITS"
    },
    {
      "type": "P",
      "id": "cytokinesis_step",
      "text": "06"
    },
    {
      "type": "Figure",
      "id": "asset_cytokinesis",
      "alt": "Two separate daughter cells, each with its own nucleus."
    },
    {
      "type": "H3",
      "id": "cytokinesis_title",
      "text": "Cytokinesis"
    },
    {
      "type": "P",
      "id": "cytokinesis_desc",
      "text": "The cytoplasm pinches in two, leaving two identical daughter cells."
    },
    {
      "type": "P",
      "id": "edge_cycle",
      "text": "each daughter cell can begin the cycle again at interphase"
    },
    {
      "type": "H2",
      "id": "panel_why_title",
      "text": "WHY IT MATTERS"
    },
    {
      "type": "P",
      "id": "panel_why_body",
      "text": "Mitosis is how a body grows, replaces worn-out cells and heals wounds. When its checks fail, cells can divide out of control, as in cancer."
    },
    {
      "type": "H2",
      "id": "panel_same_title",
      "text": "WHAT STAYS THE SAME"
    },
    {
      "type": "P",
      "id": "panel_same_body",
      "text": "Each daughter cell gets a full copy of the DNA: the same chromosomes, in the same number, as the parent cell."
    },
    {
      "type": "P",
      "id": "footer_message",
      "text": "GROW  /  COPY  /  CONDENSE  /  ALIGN  /  SEPARATE  /  SPLIT"
    },
    {
      "type": "P",
      "id": "footer_credit",
      "text": "XYZ LAYOUT ENGINE"
    },
    {
      "type": "P",
      "id": "scale_note",
      "text": "Simplified diagram: human body cells carry 46 chromosomes, not the few drawn here, and the phases blend into one another. Icons are hand-drawn vector art; the interphase and cytokinesis cells are AI-generated (GPT Image 2.5 via Higgsfield)."
    },
    {
      "type": "P",
      "id": "source_note",
      "text": "Reference: OpenStax, Biology 2e (2018), section 10.2 \"The Cell Cycle\", https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/10-2-the-cell-cycle"
    }
  ],
